omr-vps-admin (0.18+20261007) unstable; urgency=medium

  * SIP ALG: also load nf_nat_sip, the NAT half of the helper. The ct helper object only autoloads nf_conntrack_sip, and without nf_nat_sip nothing rewrites the SDP addresses of a DNATed call. Where it's missing, a warning is logged ("SIP ALG without NAT, can't load nf_nat_sip: ...") and the helper is still assigned, as it still tracks calls
  * install_omr-ai.sh: download omr_metrics.py at OMR_ADMIN_VERSION, which the VPS installer now passes, instead of always from develop, so with OMR_AI=yes omr_metrics.py matches the installed omradmin.py. Run by hand, it still takes develop
  * Check the omr-6in4/user<id> files at startup: releases before 0.18+20261005 wrote a router's ula, localip6 and remoteip6 unchecked, and omr-6in4-run applies as root, at every boot, anything that looks like an address, so a ULA=::/0 or "default" left by one router took the VPS's IPv6 default route until that router posted /vpnips again. Each file is now rewritten from what its userid stored, with the userid's own IPv6 pair, and removed with its unit when no user has its addresses (a userid nobody has). A stored ULA that isn't a /48 to /64 of fc00::/7 out of fd00::/64, or that overlaps another user's, is dropped from the config (both of two that overlap), as is a router address another router has, so that each router, missing it in /config, posts its own again
  * /vpnips: keep remoteip, where every DNAT of the user goes, to the router's own address. It was only compared with the addresses other users had stored, so a router could claim the glorytun, dsvpn, WireGuard, MQVPN or OpenVPN address of a router that hadn't posted yet, sending its ports to that router, and that router's own /vpnips was then refused. A router other than the main one is now held to the /30 of its userid in the glorytun TCP/UDP and dsvpn pools, and kept out of MLVPN's; nobody may claim another user's slot, WireGuard peer, MQVPN fixed address or live OpenVPN lease. An address another user stored but OpenVPN has since leased to the caller is the caller's: the other user's stale one is dropped, with its 6in4 and VXLAN tunnels
  * /vpnips: run under the config lock, as the checks against the other users' addresses and the writes after them are one step (two routers could claim the same address at once); a stored ULA that isn't valid (::/0 from an older release) no longer blocks every other user's; a ULA with host bits is written as its prefix, which ip route accepts; omr-6in4/user<id> is written atomically, always with the userid's own pair, and its unit enabled, as only userid 0's came back at boot
  * /config: report every user's own 6in4 pair and ULA from its omr-6in4/user<id>. Users other than userid 0 got userid 0's pair (fd00::a00:1|2) and no ULA, so their router took the other tunnel's addresses and, seeing its ULA missing, POSTed /vpnips at every run. Until a router's first /vpnips there is no file and all three stay empty, and the router keeps what it has
  * Add tests for the above (test/test_isolation.py, test/test_api.py) and move the /vpnips ownership test to userid 2's own glorytun address

