omr-vps-admin (0.18+20261006) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Keep a router's port redirects to its own traffic: the source_dip check of /firewallopen only ran when one was given, and a DNAT without source_dip matched every address of the VPS, so any read-write router could take another user's ports, on that user's dedicated IP too, or the server's own (MQVPN's udp/443, VLESS Reality's tcp/443, tcp/53...) from every user, or the whole 1-64999 range. A redirect without address now leaves out the public IPs dedicated to other users (ip daddr != { ... }), the bulk redirect too, and the main router's rules come first in user_dnat. For any router but the admin and the main one, /firewallopen refuses a DNAT overlapping another user's redirect on the same addresses ("Port already redirected by another user") and one of a port the VPS uses ("Port used by the server": what listens on more than the loopback, the V2Ray/XRay inbounds, the MQVPN port), except on the user's own dedicated IP
  * Give the MLVPN password and the external WireGuard client's private key in /config to the openmptcprouter user only: mlvpn0 is that router's instance, authenticated by the password alone, and client-wg0 is a single peer whose traffic reaches every router's tunnel, so every user of the VPS could connect as either. Other users no longer see mlvpn as an available VPN
  * Limit every request body before authentication: FastAPI reads and parses a JSON body before the authentication dependency runs, and only /backuppost had a limit, so a client without a token could make the root process parse multi-GB bodies. The limit is now 1 MiB, 16 MiB for /dscp_classify (the router's bypass lists); the upload speedtest, which streams and only counts its body once authenticated, has none
  * Report the bulk redirect state the right way round in /config: since the nftables migration redirect_ports was "disable" when the bulk redirect was on and "enable" when off, so a router with "redirect all ports" or UPnP on, which asks for "enable" at each sync, was told it had it and never got it. Such routers now get the bulk redirect they ask for
  * /wireguard: refuse a newline in a peer's addresses. Each comma-separated part was checked after strip(), and the raw value written to wg0.conf, so "10.255.247.5,\n10.255.247.6" wrote a line of its own, wg setconf failed and every later /wireguard and /remove_user answered HTTP 500. wg0.conf now gets the validated parts, and the conflict check, the store and the rewrite of wg0.conf are done under the config lock
  * Keep a revoked OpenVPN certificate revoked when its username is added again: /add_user and /config deleted every index.txt line of the name to let easyrsa reissue, so the next gen-crl dropped the old certificate from the CRL and it worked again, as the new user, and a valid one could never be revoked. A certificate still valid is now marked revoked in index.txt and the CRL regenerated; revoked lines are kept (openssl only refuses a second valid certificate for a subject). /remove_user also revokes in the index what easyrsa could not (no issued file left)
  * Read-modify-write the shared files under the config lock: /shadowsocks rewrote manager.json without it, as did add_ss_user and remove_ss_user, MQVPN's server.json was rewritten by /mqvpn, /mqvpn_user, /mqvpn_dscp, /mqvpn_weight, add_mqvpn and remove_mqvpn without one, and a user's firewall entries were read outside it, so two writers, on the two uvicorn workers, lost each other's change (a removed user's MQVPN key could come back, a new user's key or port forward be lost). /mqvpn_dscp and /mqvpn_weight take 32 interfaces at most, named [A-Za-z0-9_.@-], each one a control API round trip
  * /shadowsocks: a router's new key restarts the ss-server of its own port through the manager socket instead of ss-manager, which cut every user's connections as often as a router posted a key; a key with a '"', a '\' or a control character is refused, as OMR's ss-manager writes it unescaped into the ss-server config; when the main router changes its port, the old port's key and firewall opening are removed instead of staying open with the old key; a manager.json without port_key nor port_conf answers an error instead of HTTP 500
  * /add_user: store the shadowsocks port actually created for a user added with ips. It stored the port after it, so removing that user removed the next user's port, and two IPs or an explicit port answered HTTP 500 halfway. The first IP gets the port asked for, the others the next free ones
  * /add_user: refuse an existing username (admin and openmptcprouter included: the user was overwritten, new password and userid, its keys and ports left behind), DEFAULT (OpenVPN's fallback ccd) and names over 64 characters, a userid already used (its glorytun and dsvpn keys were regenerated) or outside 1-63 (past 63 the user was saved but its tunnels failed, its port being 650<userid>). An automatic userid past 63 takes one freed by /remove_user. /add_user and /modify_user refuse a password under 8 characters or a template one, and an empty stored password never logs in (an empty /login_basic password matched it)
  * /remove_user: remove what the user had, not just its main entries: every shadowsocks-libev port of its userid (those of its public IPs and GRE tunnels kept its key), its GRE XRay accounts and tunnel files, its OpenVPN ccd and client2client push routes, its omr6in4@ and omr-vxlan@ units and files, its MQVPN path policy and its backups, then resync the firewall and GRE SNAT chains (its DNATs stayed live until another change). A re-added name or reused userid inherited them. The admin user (no userid) can't be removed
  * Keep one user from stopping a daemon every user shares: a V2Ray redirect was only checked against V2Ray's own inbounds (and an XRay one against XRay's), so a port of the other proxy (its API inbound, Reality's tcp/443, another user's redirect) or MQVPN's made the second daemon fail to start; enabling Reality with a redirect of tcp/443 did the same. Both are now refused. A router other than the main one can't move its glorytun or dsvpn daemon to a port of the server (the API, SSH...) or of another user's tunnel, nor its VXLAN to a port the server uses (other users' VXLAN ports stay shareable), as these listen on every address and whichever started first at boot took the port
  * /dsvpn: apply a port change alone (only the key was compared, after the config was already rewritten, so a new port was saved but never used), and close the old glorytun and dsvpn ports when they change. /glorytun refuses an empty key. /openvpn and /mqvpn close the old port whoever opened it (the installer did, as the main router), /shadowsocks-go too, and /config reads the shadowsocks-go port and protocol from its server.json instead of a per-user copy only the caller of /shadowsocks-go got refreshed
  * Accept the /login_basic cookie (the API docs) on the other endpoints only from same-origin requests (Sec-Fetch-Site, or the Referer for older browsers): a browser sends it with any request to the VPS, so a link or a form on another site could call /update or a POST with the admin's rights. The docs token now expires with the cookie, after 30 minutes instead of 24 h, as any port of the VPS gets the cookie
  * /wan: at most 32 addresses per router, none wider than a /24 (IPv4) or a /48 (IPv6): they go into the shadowsocks ACL every user shares, and 0.0.0.0/0 was accepted
  * Enforce the serial number when the router sends none: with serial_enforce on, /config and /status only checked a serial that was sent. A wrong serial no longer makes a config backup, which ten requests used to rotate every dated backup away
  * Keep /status, /config and /speedtest from blocking the API: /status and /config were async but ran requests, subprocesses and file reads on the event loop, every other request of the worker waiting, and are now plain functions; /speedtest runs its MPTCP check in a thread; the OpenVPN stats reader looped at 100% CPU when the management socket closed before END; the SoftEther and shadowsocks-go API calls had no timeout; the shadowsocks-libev stats were read in 1024 bytes, which cut them past about 50 ports and made /status answer 500
  * Match the client address exactly in /mptcpsupport and the speedtest MPTCP check: a substring test found 1.2.3.4 in 11.2.3.45; the speedtest uses the cached "ss -M" of /mptcpsupport
  * Read the config the tolerant way in /token, /login_basic and the token check, as startup and every route do: a config with a trailing comma started, then every login and call answered HTTP 500
  * /softethervpn sends the password authentication type with SetUser, which replaces the whole user: without it the account could become anonymous
  * Record the one-time XRay reverse uuid rotation (xray_reverse_per_user) only once xray-server.json is written: recorded before, a failed write left the shared uuid every router had seen in place for good
  * v2ray_del_user no longer rewrites v2ray-server.json and restarts V2Ray for every user when the user had nothing there (a file with another layout always looked changed)
  * /config: match the OpenVPN port and cipher directives exactly in tun0.conf (a substring test also read comments or other directives)
  * omr_metrics: the JSON backend writes under a file lock shared by the uvicorn workers, through a unique 0600 temp file (two workers lost each other's metrics, and a half-written file made the next write wipe every user's), and keeps a corrupt file aside instead of overwriting it; the model file is saved the same way and reloaded by every worker when it changes, and only one worker runs the auto-learning
  * omr_metrics: bound what a router posts: interface names [A-Za-z0-9_.@:-] of 32 characters at most, 32 interfaces per user, no NaN or infinite values, 64-bit integers, and a timestamp more than 5 minutes off the server's replaced by the receive time. A NaN, a huge number or a forged name broke the admin's /metrics/all, /metrics/prometheus and /metrics/decision for everyone; the Prometheus labels are now escaped (an interface name could add lines for another user)
  * omr_metrics: train the shared decision model only on the admin's and the main router's metrics (any user's self-reported ones steered every user's weights), bound the torch forecasts (8 interfaces per request, 2 requests at once per worker, the linear estimate otherwise), refuse disabled users on the GET routes too, and fix the training loss, which was divided by the number of interfaces
  * omr_metrics_standalone and docker-compose: refuse to start without a real OMR_PASS (it defaulted to "changeme") and publish the port on 127.0.0.1 unless OMR_BIND says otherwise
  * omr-admin.service: NoNewPrivileges=yes and ProtectHome=yes (nothing it runs needs a setuid binary or a home directory; checked on the test VPS)
  * CI: read-only token permissions, actions/checkout@v4, and the deploy actions pinned to a commit
  * Add tests for the above (test/test_hardening.py, test/test_metrics.py) and adapt the firewall, MQVPN, dsvpn, user and XRay reverse tests to the new behaviour

