omr-vps-admin (0.18+20261005) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Send the MQVPN server's key pin in /config (mqvpn.pinned_pubkey, GHSA-qq6x-5r9f-2w3m): the router ran mqvpn with Insecure, which verifies no certificate, so an attacker on the path to the VPS could stand up its own mqvpn server, receive the user's MQVPN key from the router and replay it to the real server. mqvpn_server_pin() returns the base64 SHA-256 of the SubjectPublicKeyInfo of the certificate server.json names (the value the installer's omr_api_pin gives), cached on its mtime and size, and the router passes it to mqvpn as PinnedPubkey so that only a server holding that key gets the key. The pin travels over the pinned API channel that already carries the key. Empty when the certificate is missing or unreadable
  * Keep omr-admin-config.json owner-only (openmptcprouter#4385): the file holds every user's password and the VPN keys and the installer hardens it to 0600, but the config writers recreated it from a temp file written with the process umask, so a toggle or a resync left it 0644 (world-readable), and the main writer then preserved that mode for good. _write_omr_config_unlocked now always writes it 0600, which also repairs a file widened by another writer, and both it and omr_metrics._auto_set_enabled create their temp copy 0600 through an open() opener instead of chmod-ing it after the secrets were already written. At startup a world-readable live file is tightened right away rather than at the next config change
  * Stop the config backup rotation evicting the installer's pre-upgrade omr-admin-config.json.bak: its .* glob matched that recovery copy, which was gone within ten config writes. backup_config now rotates only its own timestamped backups (.[0-9]*) and makes each one 0600, and at startup the existing backups (.bak*, .<epoch>) left world-readable by older releases or by the installer's mv of a widened live file are chmod-ed 0600
  * omr_metrics._auto_set_enabled no longer rewrites a user-less config when the file is missing: it reports that persistence failed and the toggle still applies at runtime
  * Recover from a backup at startup instead of crash-looping: the config was read with a bare json.load, so an empty, truncated or hand-edited file (a trailing comma, or no users) made omr-admin raise at import and, with Restart=always and omr-service restarting it whenever the API is unreachable, crash-loop without ever using the backups it keeps. The new load_startup_config() parses the live file the tolerant way the runtime reader does and, if it is missing, empty, unparsable or has lost its users, recovers from the newest usable backup (the installer's .bak and the timestamped copies) and writes it back so later changes don't fail in turn. If nothing is usable it fails with one clear message rather than starting with no users, which would reject every router
  * Report Pi-hole v6 as installed in /config: pihole.state was set only when /etc/pihole/setupVars.conf exists, but Pi-hole v6 keeps its settings in /etc/pihole/pihole.toml and its migration from v5 moves setupVars.conf away, so a VPS whose Pi-hole was updated to v6 reported pihole.state false and the router stopped sending its DNS queries to it. Either file now counts as installed, as Pi-hole's own installer does; "pihole uninstall" removes /etc/pihole, so an uninstalled Pi-hole is still reported as not installed
  * Refuse redirecting (DNAT) ports from 65000 up on /firewallopen and /shorewallopen: they carry the server's own services (Glorytun 65001, Shadowsocks 65101, SSH 65222, the API 65500...), so a redirect there sent them to the router and locked the VPS out. The router already refuses them and the /shorewall bulk redirect stops at 64999; the API now answers "Ports >= 65000 can't be redirected" instead of applying it. Opening (ACCEPT) them stays allowed
  * Fix an authenticated nftables command injection in the firewall API (GHSA-p7h3-26vj-4wg3, reported by Pierre-Yves Le Borgne): validate every firewall entry before it reaches nft. Port, proto, source_dip and source_ip went verbatim into the script fed to `nft -f -`, one command per line, so a value carrying a newline added nft commands of its own and anything else malformed made the single user_accept/user_dnat flush fail, dropping every user's ports. The port must be a port or an a-b / a:b range within 1-65535 (a:b is rendered as nft's a-b), proto one of tcp, udp, sctp and the addresses plain IP or CIDR literals (ipaddress also takes an IPv6 scope ID such as fe80::1%<anything>, newlines included, and keeps it in str(), so a new _ip_network() refuses any character outside [0-9A-Fa-f:./]; /dscp_classify and its startup replay use it too, as their cidr went into the nft set element list the same way); /firewallopen answers 'Invalid port', 'Invalid protocol' or 'Invalid address', _fw_port_add refuses such entries for every caller and _render_fw_ports skips and logs any stored by an earlier version. nft rule comments also lose control characters and backslashes, so a name or comment can no longer break out of its line
  * Redirect port ranges with V2Ray and XRay (vps#85): the router sends a range forward's port and destport as a-b, and v2ray_add_port/xray_add_port did int() on them, so /v2rayredirect and /xrayredirect answered HTTP 500 and the forward was never opened. XRay now gets one range inbound with destination port 0, which its dokodemo-door replaces by the port each connection came in on; V2Ray has no such fallback and would dial port 0, so there (and for a range moved to another range, on both) each port gets an inbound of its own, tagged <tag>#<port> and removed together by the unredirect. A range sent to a single port stays one inbound. Both endpoints now refuse an invalid port, protocol, address or destination port, ports from 65000 up, a destination range of another width, and ranges over 1024 ports (a DMZ is sent as 2-64999: v2ray and xray listen on every port of a range, and a single port in use stops the daemon for every user) with a reason instead of writing them to the config
  * Restart V2Ray/XRay once per router sync instead of once per changed forward: each restart drops every proxied connection of every user, and the router sends its forwards one request at a time. A change now marks a restart pending in /run, shared by the uvicorn workers, and the daemon is restarted once no redirect changed for 5 seconds; a restart left pending by a stopped omr-admin is resumed at startup. A redirect the router re-sends unchanged, and an unredirect of nothing, no longer rewrite the config at all
  * Write the daemon configs atomically: v2ray-server.json, xray-server.json, xray-vless-reality.json, mqvpn's server.json, shadowsocks-libev's manager.json and local.acl, shadowsocks-go's server.json, omr-bypass.json, current-vpn and current-proxy were truncated then rewritten in place, so a crash (the OOM killer on a small VPS), a full disk or an exception halfway through left an empty or half-written file, which the daemon then failed to parse at its next restart, for every user. The new _atomic_write_json()/_atomic_write_text() write a temp file next to it, fsync it and rename it over the old one, so a reader sees the old file or the new one, never a part. The temp copy is created 0600 and then gets the mode and owner of the file it replaces (the installer's 0600 on xray-server.json is kept; a new file is 0600, or 0644 for the text files as before), and the config.json symlinks are followed rather than replaced. The redirect paths also do their read-modify-write under the config lock, so two uvicorn workers can't lose each other's change
  * Write the tunnel files atomically too: the glorytun and dsvpn configs and keys (new user, /glorytun, /dsvpn), the OpenVPN ccd/<user> iroutes (/lan) and the GRE tunnel files were written line by line in place, so a crash or an exception halfway left a tunnel with an empty key or a config missing its last lines. They are now built in memory and written through _atomic_write_text(). The /glorytun and /dsvpn updates already went through a mkstemp() copy, but in /tmp, a tmpfs on many VPS, so move() could not rename it into /etc and copied it over the config in place. A GRE tunnel file is only written when missing, so one cut off by an earlier release now counts as missing (no USERID= line) and is rewritten
  * Keep the glorytun and dsvpn keys 0600: each user's key was written with the process umask (0644, world-readable) and the installer never hardened dsvpn0.key. New keys are created 0600, and at startup omr-admin chmods 0600 any existing world-readable /etc/glorytun-{tcp,udp}/tun*.key and /etc/dsvpn/dsvpn*.key (their daemons run as root)
  * Stop rewriting files through a mkstemp() copy in /tmp: /tmp is a tmpfs on many VPS, so move() could not rename the copy into /etc and copied it over the target in place, truncating it first. The OpenVPN tun0.conf (client-to-client, /openvpn, /lan), wg0.conf (which holds the server's private key) and the sysctl.d files (/mptcp and the startup normalization) are now built in memory and written through _atomic_write_text(); tempfile.mkstemp is no longer used
  * Fix /dsvpn always answering "DSVPN is not installed" and /vpn_list never listing dsvpn: both checked /etc/dsvpn/dsvpn, which the installer does not create (it creates dsvpn0 and moves dsvpn.key to dsvpn0.key). They now check dsvpn0, as the rest of omr-admin does, and /dsvpn for a user whose dsvpn<id> was never created answers "DSVPN is not set up for this user" instead of an HTTP 500
  * /glorytun now restarts glorytun when only the key changed: it compared the configs alone, so a new key was not used until the next restart. Each of glorytun-tcp and glorytun-udp is also updated only if this user has it, so a VPS with only one of them installed no longer answers HTTP 500
  * Removing a user no longer leaves its tunnels behind: the glorytun-tcp config (from which /config kept reading the removed user's addresses) is deleted with its key, and the gt-udp-tun<id> interface, which outlives glorytun-udp because of its "persist" option, is deleted with the service. The remove functions also refuse userid 0, whose tun0/dsvpn0 are the templates every user's config is built from
  * Add unit tests for the V2Ray/XRay range redirects, their validation, the shared restart batching, the atomic writer, the tunnel files and keys, the /dsvpn and /vpn_list checks, the /glorytun restarts, the tunnel removal, and a check that none of these configs is written in place any more (test/test_api.py); under the test mocks the writer goes through open(path, 'w') as before, so the existing tests still see what is written
  * Keep the firewall sync and omr-admin's startup from failing for every user on one entry: an IPv6 (or "IPv4 and IPv6") port redirect of a user created by /add_user, which stores the userid as a string, made '{:x}' raise in the renderer, so every later port change of every user answered HTTP 500 and nothing reached user_accept/user_dnat; and as the startup resync is not guarded, omr-admin then no longer started at all until the config was edited by hand. The tunnel address is now computed from int(userid) (none past userid 255, where it would get five hex digits), each entry is rendered on its own and one that fails is skipped and logged, a redirect target that is not an address of the rule's family is skipped, and an exception in the startup resync is logged instead of stopping the process
  * Keep values nft refuses out of the user_accept/user_dnat transaction: a rule comment is now printable ASCII only, so that 128 characters are 128 bytes (120 x "é" passed the [:128] cut at 240 bytes, which nft refuses) and a lone surrogate from the JSON body can no longer make the script fail to encode; and the address check refuses the netmask and hostmask forms (1.2.3.4/255.255.255.0, 1.2.3.4/0.0.0.255) and prefixes with leading zeros (nft reads /024 as octal), which Python's ipaddress accepts and nft does not
  * Redirect (DNAT) only what reaches the VPS from the internet, as Shorewall's "DNAT net vpn:..." did: the nft rules matched in nat_prerouting on every interface, so a user's redirect of udp/53 or tcp/443 also took the new connections every other router opens through its own tunnel, to any destination, and sent them to that user; the bulk redirect did the same for ports 1-64999. Every DNAT rule now starts with iifname != { the VPN interfaces, client-wg* }. /firewallopen also refuses a source_dip that is the public IP another user has to itself (its /add_user ips)
  * Refuse a V2Ray/XRay redirect of a port the daemon already listens on: only an identical redirect was recognised, so a second router forwarding the same port, a forward moved to another destination, or tcp/443 with VLESS Reality on added a second listener, and v2ray/xray failed to start for every user. A port of another user's redirect or of the server's own inbounds (the proxies, Reality, the API inbound) is answered "Port already in use on the server"; the same user's redirect of that port to another destination is replaced, which is how the router moves a forward (it removes the old one afterwards); a user can redirect 4096 ports at most. /v2rayunredirect and /xrayunredirect now check their values too: the redirect tag starts with the username, so a free proto or destip could spell the tag of another user whose name starts with the caller's
  * Give each router its own V2Ray/XRay reverse tunnel: every user's redirects went to the single OMRLan tunnel and /config gave every router the uuid of its XRay client, so any router connecting its bridge got a share of the forwarded connections of every user. The main router (userid 0) keeps OMRLan; any other user gets OMRLan-<username>, an XRay reverse client of its own (email omr-reverse@<username>, its uuid in /config xray.config.reverse_key) or a V2Ray portal of its own, reached by a routing rule on the email (or socks account) the router's bridge logs in with, and the shared V2Ray portal now only takes the main router's bridge. This is set up at startup and when a user is added, the redirects of the other users are moved to their tunnel, and on a VPS with more than one user the uuid of the shared XRay client, which every router has seen, is replaced once (global flag xray_reverse_per_user). /config creates none for a user without an XRay client (the admin user). Removing a user removes its tunnel and its redirects. The router only reloaded reverse_key when another XRay key changed, and only on a forced retrieve: on a VPS with more than one user, XRay forwards need the matching openmptcprouter-vps change (which takes a new reverse_key at every sync) to come back. Both configs checked with "v2ray test" (5.32) and "xray run -test" (26.7) on the test VPS
  * Limit the VXLAN tunnel addresses to the VXLAN pools: /vxlan accepted any address with any prefix and omr-vxlan-run adds LOCALTUNIP to the device as is, so 1.0.0.1/1 installed a 0.0.0.0/1 route over it, taking half of the VPS's IPv4 away from every user. localip/remoteip must now be a /30 of 10.255.249.0/24 or 10.255.224.0/20 and localip6/remoteip6 a /126 of fd00::b00:0/108 or fd00::b000:0/100 (the slices the defaults come from), a localip slice another user has (as set, or by default) is refused, and a stored value outside them is no longer written
  * Keep /vpnips to the router's own addresses: omr-6in4-run routes the ULA prefix via the router as root ("ip route replace ${ULA} via ..."), so ::/0 replaced the VPS's IPv6 default route, and remoteip is what every DNAT of the user goes to. ula must now be a ULA (fc00::/7) /48 to /64 outside fd00::/64 (the VPS's own tunnels) and not overlapping another user's, localip6/remoteip6 can only be the user's own fd00::a0<id>:1/2 pair, and remoteip can't be another router's address or the VPS end of any tunnel. Read-only users, the default of /add_user, can still use it
  * Keep WireGuard peers to the WireGuard VPN: wg-quick routes every AllowedIPs of wg0 over it, and only another user's addresses were refused, so a peer claiming 0.0.0.0/0, a public address or another tunnel's pool took the VPS's traffic or brought wg0 down for everyone. A peer must now be one router address of 10.255.247.0/24 (not .0, .1 the server, nor .255); the others are dropped, not refused, since the router sends the address of each of its WireGuard interfaces, those to other servers too. Stored peers outside it are no longer written to wg0.conf
  * Validate the /lan networks before storing them: with client2client each one becomes an iroute in the router's ccd file and a route pushed to every OpenVPN client, and nothing was checked, so 0.0.0.0/0 sent every client's traffic to that router, and one entry netaddr could not parse made /lan fail for every user from then on. A LAN must now be an IPv4 network (ipaddr/netmask, ipaddr/prefix or ipaddr/prefix/); with client2client on, it must also be private (RFC 1918 or 100.64.0.0/10) outside 10.255.0.0/16, and one overlapping another user's LAN is answered "conflict" instead of taking that user's traffic. Stored entries that can't be routed are left out of the ccd files and push routes
  * Keep what every user shares to the administrator and the main router (userid 0, the installer's user, which has no admin rights): any read-write router could change and restart, for every user, the shadowsocks-libev method and options, the shadowsocks-go listener, the XRay transport, method and Reality, MPTCP and the TCP sysctls, the DSCP sets and BPF maps, the bulk redirect, SIP ALG, the mqvpn, OpenVPN, mlvpn and SoftEther server settings, omr-bypass's list, and run /update (which reinstalls and reboots the VPS). These now answer "permission" to any other router; /shadowsocks still sets the key of its own port, /softethervpn its own password, and /vpn and /proxy its own choice (current-vpn and current-proxy, which omr-service watches, are the main router's). /wan now stores each router's WAN IPs and writes all of them to local.acl, instead of the last router's only. /xray holds the config lock, as the redirects that also rewrite xray-server.json do
  * /shadowsocks: refuse the port of another user. It took over that port's key and recorded the port as the caller's, so removing the caller then removed the other user's port; a port missing from port_conf answered HTTP 500. A router other than the main one keeps the port /add_user gave it
  * /mlvpn: refuse a password with a newline, a quote, a space or a '%' (configparser wrote a newline as continuation lines of mlvpn0.conf and raised on a '%'), and write mlvpn0.conf atomically, 0600
  * Send the shadowsocks-libev manager its add command with json.dumps: the key was pasted between quotes, so a '"' in it changed the command
  * /mptcpsupport, which needs no login, now reads /proc/net/mptcp_net/mptcp or runs "ss -M" at most once every 2 seconds whatever the number of requests, outside the event loop: each request ran "ss -M" on the event loop, so a flood stalled the API of every router
  * Tie the API tokens to the password they were given for: a token stayed valid for its 24 h after the password was changed, or after the user was removed and added again. The new pwd claim is an HMAC of the password with the server's secret key; tokens without it are refused, so routers log in again once. A password that isn't ASCII is now a wrong password instead of an HTTP 500
  * Backups: /backupget, /backuplist and the /backuppost rotation matched <username>-*, so user "foo" could read, list and delete the backups of user "foo-bar"; they now match <username>-backup.tar.gz and <username>-<time>-backup.tar.gz exactly, a name being the longest username it can belong to (bob-1-backup.tar.gz is user bob-1's, not a copy of bob's). A backup is limited to 16 MiB (the request body is refused with a 413 as it comes in, before it is decoded) and written 0600, as is the config backup, now created 0600 instead of copied with the umask and chmod-ed
  * Match usernames exactly in the OpenVPN PKI index cleanup of /config and /add_user (a substring test removed the entries of every user whose name starts with the one being created) and in the OpenVPN traffic counters (which returned another user's, or answered HTTP 500 on a header line)
  * Refuse a non-admin user without a userid instead of taking it for userid 0: /status and /config then returned the main router's config and keys, and /vxlan, /glorytun, /dsvpn and /vpnips wrote its tunnel files. /add_user always gives one and the installer's user is 0, so only a hand-edited config has such a user. The admin user, which has none, still acts as the main router there (a router logged in with it is the main one), and its V2Ray/XRay redirects keep the main router's reverse tunnel
  * Stop shipping omr-admin-config.json in /etc (and as a conffile): the live config, rewritten by omr-admin all the time, is then never offered for replacement by the package's template on an upgrade, which put MySecretKey/AdminMySecretKey back and dropped every user and key when accepted, and a package installed without the installer no longer starts with the template's passwords. The template stays in /usr/share/omr-admin, which the installer copies when the file is missing; an existing config is kept by dpkg (an obsolete conffile, only removed on purge)
  * Refuse to log in a user whose password is still the config template's (MySecretKey, AdminMySecretKey), which anyone can read in the repository: the installer replaces them, so only a VPS whose installation stopped halfway, or a package installed without it, still has them. /token and /login_basic answer as for a wrong password, a token is refused, and omr-admin names those users in a warning at startup
  * Run uvicorn with proxy_headers=False: no proxy sits in front of the API, and the X-Forwarded-For of a connection from 127.0.0.1, which a router can open through the VPS's own proxies, set the client address log_auth_failure() hands fail2ban
  * Ignore the omr-admin binary cypthon.py leaves at the root of the source tree
  * Add tests for the above (test/test_isolation.py, test/test_nft.py), and adapt the /wan, /wireguard, /vxlan and DNAT rendering tests to the new behaviour
  * Validate the /mptcp values before persisting them: checksum, path_manager, scheduler and congestion_control were written verbatim as key=value lines into /etc/sysctl.d/90-shadowsocks.conf, applied as root at every boot, so a newline in one of them persisted any other kernel setting. checksum must now be 0 or 1 and the three others a kernel name ([A-Za-z0-9_-], 32 characters at most); anything else is answered 'Invalid <field>' before any sysctl is run
  * Validate the /wan IPs before writing them: they went verbatim into /etc/shadowsocks-libev/local.acl, shared by every user of the VPS, so a request could add ACL rules of its own. Each line must now be a plain IPv4/IPv6 address or CIDR, otherwise the API answers 'Invalid IP' and leaves the file untouched
  * Refuse values that could run commands as root through the files omr-admin writes for root scripts and daemons:
    - /vpnips: localip6, remoteip6 and ula were written verbatim into omr-6in4/user<id>, which omr-6in4-run sourced as root at the restart of omr6in4@user<id> that follows, and the route takes read-only users too. localip6/remoteip6 must now be IPv6 addresses (with or without a /prefix) and ula an IPv6 prefix or "auto", otherwise the API answers 'Invalid <field>' before writing anything
    - /vxlan: localip, remoteip, localip6 and remoteip6 were stored and written as LOCALTUNIP/LOCALTUNIP6 into omr-vxlan/user<id>, sourced by omr-vxlan-run as root. They must now be addresses of their family, and write_vxlan_conf leaves out (and logs) a stored value that isn't, so one saved by an earlier release is not written again at the next /vxlan or /vpnips call
    - /openvpn: the cipher was written verbatim into the shared tun0.conf, so a newline added any OpenVPN directive, script hooks run as root included. It must now be a cipher name ([A-Za-z0-9-], 64 characters at most), otherwise 'Invalid cipher'
    - /wireguard: a peer's key and ip were written verbatim into wg0.conf, so a newline could add an [Interface] section whose hooks wg-quick runs as root. The key must now be a WireGuard key (base64 of 32 bytes) and ip an address or prefix, or a comma-separated list of them, otherwise 'Invalid key' / 'Invalid ip'
    - openmptcprouter-vps's omr-6in4-run and omr-vxlan-run no longer source these files either
  * /wireguard no longer drops the other users' peers: every call rewrote the shared wg0.conf with the caller's peers only, so on a VPS with several users each router's sync cut the tunnels of all the others. Each user's peers are now kept in its own 'wireguard_peers' and wg0.conf is rebuilt from every user's, keeping its [Interface]. A peer with another user's key, or an address overlapping another user's peer, is refused ('Key already used by another user', 'Address already used by another user'): WireGuard gives an address to the last peer claiming it, so it would have taken that user's traffic. Peers no user owns yet, those an earlier release left in wg0.conf, are dropped at the first call, and each router puts its own back at its next sync, as it re-posts them every time. /remove_user also rebuilds wg0.conf when the removed user had peers, instead of leaving them there until another user's next call
  * Add unit tests for the config and backup permissions, the backup rotation sparing the installer's .bak, the startup recovery from a backup, the Pi-hole v6 detection (test/test_api.py, test/test_metrics.py) the firewall entry validation (test/test_api.py, test/test_nft.py, test/test_dscp_sync.py) the /mptcp, /wan, /vpnips, /openvpn and /wireguard validation, the per-user WireGuard peers (test/test_api.py) and the /vxlan one (test/test_vxlan.py)

