omr-vps-admin (0.18+20260916) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix concurrent writes to omr-admin-config.json losing each other's changes and, in the worst case, leaving the file truncated so omr-admin no longer starts: every mutation (set_global_param, modif_config_user, set_lastchange, check_username_serial, /add_user, /remove_user) read the whole config, edited its own copy and json.dump'ed it straight over the live file with no lock and no atomicity, while omr-admin runs two uvicorn workers plus the metrics engine's background threads and the router pushes several endpoints in the same second. Two overlapping requests meant the second write silently reverted the first (a /shorewallopen landing during the router's status/lastchange push), and a reader or a crash hitting the middle of the dump saw a half-written file. All of them now go through a single _mutate_omr_config() which takes an exclusive flock on /etc/openmptcprouter-vps-admin/.omr-admin-config.lock (plus a re-entrant thread lock), re-reads the config under that lock so the change applies to the newest content instead of a snapshot taken before it, and replaces the file through a temp file + fsync + rename that preserves its mode, so on-disk it is either the old config or the new one and never a partial one
  * read_omr_config() takes the same lock shared, so a reader can no longer observe a file being replaced, and returns {} on OSError (missing or unreadable file) instead of letting it propagate to callers that only expected a parse error
  * /add_user and /remove_user hold the exclusive lock for the whole request (_serialise_config_write): both read the config at entry, then spend seconds generating or revoking Glorytun, OpenVPN, Shadowsocks, v2ray/xray and MQVPN material before writing it back, so anything persisted in that window was overwritten with the pre-request snapshot -- two /add_user calls in a row could leave only the second user in the file. Their final write also merges the user into the latest config rather than into that stale copy
  * check_username_serial() no longer raises KeyError (a 500 on /status with a serial) for a username absent from the config, returns False instead, and persists a first-seen serial under the lock with a backup like every other mutation
  * Fix GET /status answering 500 for a user that does not exist: /status?username=<unknown> got the {'error': 'Unknown user'} dict back from get_userid_from_username() and passed it on as a userid, and any unknown userid resolved to an empty username, so the handler reached omr_config_data['users'][0][username] and died on a KeyError after having collected the whole VPS status. Both lookups are checked and answer {'error': 'Unknown user', 'route': 'status'}
  * Fix POST /backuppost destroying the stored backup when the router posts a truncated or non-base64 body: <user>-backup.tar.gz and the timestamped copy were both opened for writing, truncating the existing backup to 0 bytes, before base64.b64decode() ran, so a decode error left the user with no backup at all and a 500. The payload is now decoded and rejected (invalid base64, or empty once decoded) before any file is opened
  * omr_metrics.py's auto_learning toggle writes the config under the same lock file as omr-admin and through a pid/thread unique temp name instead of the fixed omr-admin-config.json.tmp shared by every worker (two toggles at once could rename a half-written temp over the config), fsyncs it, and removes the temp file if the write fails
  * install_omr-ai.sh takes the same lock around its own read-modify-write of omr-admin-config.json, since it is meant to be run on a live VPS and otherwise dropped whatever the API had persisted since it read the file
  * Fix install_omr-ai.sh reporting "Database ready." and carrying on with no database when the influxdb3 create database call failed for any reason: its output went through `grep -v "already exists" || true`, which swallowed a refused connection, a rejected token or a bad retention value just as well. Only an "already exists" reply is treated as success now, any other failure prints the CLI output and aborts the install
  * POST /vxlan and POST /vxlan_user answer with the configuration they just applied instead of re-reading omr-admin-config.json outside the lock, which could echo back a concurrent change
  * Add unit tests for the concurrent config mutations (20 threads, every update preserved), the unknown user paths of /status, /backuppost refusing invalid base64 before opening any file (test/test_api.py), and the database creation step aborting on a real failure while still accepting an existing database (test/test_install_omr_ai.py)

