omr-vps-admin (0.18+20260908) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Log a failed login where fail2ban can count it, and only when it is really one: new log_auth_failure() emits "omr-admin: authentication failure from <ip>" at WARNING for a rejected credential pair on POST /token and GET /login_basic. Until now nothing logged a failed login with the client address (authenticate_user() only had LOG.debug lines with no IP, invisible at log_level='info'), so the fail2ban omradmin jail had to parse uvicorn's access log for "POST /token" 400 and "GET /login_basic" 401 -- and those two statuses also cover a router that has no VPS key yet (fresh flash or mid-wizard) polling /token with an empty password, and a browser drawing the Basic-auth challenge on its first /docs request. Routers consequently banned themselves out of the API they were being configured from (the ban rejects tcp/65500, so the router only reported "can ping server vps, no server API answer"), and six /docs visits banned the operator. The marker is therefore skipped when either half of the pair is empty and when there is no Authorization header at all, which only omr-admin can tell apart; HTTP responses are unchanged (400/token, 401/login_basic), and the matching filter ships in openmptcprouter-vps (fail2ban-filter-omradmin.conf, needs this version or newer)
  * log_auth_failure() logs the peer address and nothing else on purpose: a username echoed into that line could carry a newline and forge a second line matching the filter with any <HOST> in it, which would turn the jail into a way to have arbitrary addresses banned
  * Stop logging a few dozen nft "Error: Could not process rule: No such file or directory" lines at every startup that happens before the base ruleset is loaded: on a VPS install/update our own deb restarts omr-admin from its postinst while /etc/nftables.conf hasn't been loaded yet, so the whole startup resync (user_accept, user_dnat, gre_snat, client2client, the 10 DSCP set declarations, dscp_mark) fails against a table inet omr that doesn't exist, once per uvicorn process. Nothing was lost -- nftables.service's omr-admin-resync drop-in restarts omr-admin right after the load and that pass lands the state -- so _nft_resync_all() now checks the table is there and otherwise skips the firewall resync with a single INFO line saying it runs again on the nftables.service restart; /etc/openvpn/tun0.conf's client-to-client sync isn't nftables state and keeps running either way
  * _nft_run() reports a failed apply as debug instead of warning when the base table is absent (probed with `nft list table inet omr` only on failure), so a firewall change pushed by the router in that same window no longer warns about something the queued restart already fixes
  * Collapse nft's stderr into one journal line: nft prints three lines per problem (diagnostic, the offending input line echoed back, a caret line) and a single script can carry a problem per element, so one failed apply of the DSCP set declarations alone was 30+ lines; identical diagnostics are now deduplicated with an "(xN)" count and their file:line:col prefix stripped
  * Report a kernel without MPTCP BPF scheduler support once instead of a multi-line libbpf warning per shipped object per uvicorn worker (8 objects x 2 workers at every startup on a VPS not yet rebooted into the OMR kernel): the "not found in kernel BTF" / "not found in kernel or module BTFs" failures are collected and logged as one INFO line listing the skipped schedulers, since that's a property of the running kernel and not of the files; any other load failure still warns, now on a single line
  * Add unit tests for the missing-base-table paths (the warning/debug split in _nft_run, _nft_resync_all skipping the nft syncs while still syncing tun0.conf) and the stderr summary (test/test_nft.py), and for the BPF-unsupported detection (test/test_api.py)

