omr-vps-admin (0.18+20260906) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Add GET /mqvpn_users (admin only) listing MQVPN users with, per name, whether it is persisted in /etc/mqvpn/server.json (with its fixed_ip), known by the running daemon (control socket list_users) and currently connected (get_status); the three can differ since add_mqvpn() persists a user even when the live add_user call fails and the daemon only learns it on restart, which now shows up as configured but not known. Keys are not returned; known/connected are null and control.error is set when the control socket is unreachable
  * mqvpn_api() connects to the address in server.json's control_listen (HOST:PORT or [V6]:PORT, wildcard binds reached via loopback) instead of a hardcoded 127.0.0.1:9090, which remains the fallback when the key is missing or malformed
  * Add unit tests for /mqvpn_users and the control_listen parsing (test/test_api.py); document /mqvpn_users in docs/api.md
  * Fix POST /vxlan_user force=true merging two users onto the same VNI while both stayed on the default UDP port 4789: Linux refuses a second VXLAN device with the same VNI on the same dstport whatever the local/remote addresses ("A VXLAN device with the specified VNI already exists"), so the second user's omr-vxlan@ unit failed at ip link add and that user had no tunnel at all. /vxlan_user now also takes a port; a VNI+port pair already held by another user is rejected regardless of force, which only bypasses the plain shared-VNI warning (the one exception is a request that merely disables an already colliding user); POST /vxlan applies the same pair check when a user enables the tunnel or changes their own port; GET /vxlan_vnis lists the port next to the VNI; vni (0-16777215) and port (1-65535) are range-checked on both models
  * Fix the userid-derived VXLAN L3 default addresses: 10.255.249.<userid*4+1>/30 overflowed past .255 from userid 64 on and fd00::b0<hex(userid)> grew a fifth hex digit from userid 256 on. Userids 0-63 keep their 10.255.249.0/24 slice, 64-1087 continue into 10.255.224.0/20 (unused by any other OMR component), 256-4095 use fd00::b<hex>:x (disjoint from the historical b0<hex> groups); past those pools no default is derived, write_vxlan_conf() omits the LOCALTUNIP lines with a warning and localip/localip6 must be set explicitly
  * GET /mqvpn_users no longer lists sessions authenticated with the server-wide auth_key, which the daemon's get_status reports as user "(global)", as a connected user; they are counted in a new top-level global_key_clients field (null when get_status is unavailable)
  * Add unit tests for the VNI+port pair check on /vxlan and /vxlan_user, the port field, the id range checks and the derived address pools (test/test_vxlan.py) and for the "(global)" handling (test/test_api.py); document the pair rule, the port and the default address pools in docs/api.md
  * Fix install_omr-ai.sh failing on every fresh install with current influxdb3-core (3.10/3.11) at the admin token bootstrap step (Ysurac/openmptcprouter#4366): the CLI parses "create token --admin" as its own sub-command and rejects "--host ... --admin" with "unexpected argument '--host' found", and since stderr was discarded the empty result was reported as "An admin token exists in the InfluxDB 3 catalog but /etc/influxdb3/omr-influxdb.env is missing or has no token"; the flags are now ordered "--admin --host ... --format json" (accepted by every 3.x release), only the "token name already exists" reply (printed by the CLI on stdout with exit 0) is treated as an existing token, and any other failure is shown verbatim instead
  * install_omr-ai.sh honours RESET_DATA, INSTALL_AI, INFLUX_RETENTION and INFLUX_RETENTION_DAYS from the environment: the error text told users to re-run with RESET_DATA="true" but the script unconditionally reset it to "false", so `sudo RESET_DATA=true sh install_omr-ai.sh` ran into the same error; the wipe path waits for the restarted server and re-checks the bootstrap result instead of dying with a generic message after the data is gone
  * install_omr-ai.sh writes /etc/influxdb3/omr-influxdb.env right after the token is created instead of at the very end, so a failure in the later pip/PyTorch/download steps no longer loses a token that is never shown again and leaves the next run in the "token exists but creds file missing" dead end
  * Add unit tests for install_omr-ai.sh (test/test_install_omr_ai.py): the admin-token bootstrap section is extracted from the shipped script and run under sh against fake influxdb3/systemctl/curl binaries that reproduce the 3.10/3.11 CLI behaviour (legacy flag order rejected, token JSON on a fresh catalog, "token name already exists" with exit 0 otherwise), covering the flag order, the environment overrides of RESET_DATA/INSTALL_AI/INFLUX_RETENTION*, the explicit "creds lost" refusal, the RESET_DATA=true wipe sequence and the verbatim reporting of other CLI errors; conftest gains a real_env marker to opt such tests out of the autouse open/subprocess mocks

